The short answer is yes, you can , but you generally shouldn't use a traditional web scraper (like Selenium or BeautifulSoup) to crawl their live site. Wikipedia strictly regulates bot traffic to protect their non-profit servers. If you try to scrape thousands of pages, your IP will likely be blocked. Instead, Wikipedia provides two official, highly efficient methods to get their data without scraping. 1. The "Right" Way: Database Dumps This is the gold standard for bulk data. If you need a massive amount of data (e.g., "all physics articles" or "the entire English Wikipedia"), do not crawl the website. Download the entire database. What it is: A complete snapshot of Wikipedia's content (articles, templates, media links). Cost: Free. Where to get it: dumps.wikimedia.org Pros: No rate limits, no fear of IP bans, faster processing (you are parsing local files, not making network requests). Format: Typically massive XML or SQL files (often compressed as .bz2 ). You will likely need a stream parser (like mwxml in Python) to read them because they are too large to load into RAM. 2. The API Method (For Specific Data) If you only need specific, real-time data (e.g., "get the summary of the 'Apple Inc.' page"), use the official API rather than scraping HTML. What it is: A structured way to query data. Endpoint: https://en.wikipedia.org/w/api.php Libraries: Python: wikipedia-api or wikipedi-api wrappers exist, but requests works fine. Rule: You must identify yourself. Set a custom User-Agent header with your contact info so they can contact you if your bot malfunctions. headers = {
    'User-Agent': 'MyCoolBot/1.0 (myemail@example.com)'
} Comparison: Why you shouldn't scrape Feature Web Scraping (BeautifulSoup/Selenium) Database Dumps Public API Speed Slow (limited by HTTP requests) Fastest (Local file I/O) Fast (for small batches) Reliability Low (HTML structure changes break code) High (Stable XML schema) High (Versioned) Risk High (IP Ban risk) Zero Low (If rules followed) Use Case Not recommended Training AI, Offline Analysis Chatbots, Live Lookups Summary If you are building a small project (less than ~1 request per second), use the API .
If you are building a dataset, AI model, or analytics engine, use the Database Dumps .

It is unusual for reqres.in to return a 403 Forbidden as it is a public test API, but this usually happens for one of two reasons: Missing "User-Agent": The server thinks you are a bot/script it doesn't trust. Network Firewall: If you are on a university or corporate network (like the CIRAT lab), the firewall might be blocking the domain. Here is the quickest fix to get your demonstration working right now. Option 1: The Quick Fix (Add a Header) In Thunder Client, some servers block requests that don't identify themselves. Try adding a "User-Agent" to trick the server into thinking you are a regular web browser. Go to your Request in Thunder Client. Click the Headers tab. In the Header field type: User-Agent In the Value field type: Mozilla/5.0 Click Send again. Option 2: The Better Alternative (JSONPlaceholder) If Option 1 doesn't work, reqres.in is likely blocked on your network. Switch to JSONPlaceholder . It is the "gold standard" for testing because it is extremely permissive and rarely blocked. Use this URL instead: https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com/users Revised Demo Steps for JSONPlaceholder: Method: GET URL: https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com/users Send: Click Send. Result: You will see a list of 10 fake users (Leanne Graham, Ervin Howell, etc.). For the POST demo: URL: https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com/users (Same URL, just change method to POST). Body (JSON): {
  "name": "CIRAT Demo User",
  "email": "demo@cirat.edu"
} Result: You will get a status 201 Created and an id: 11 . Why this is actually better for your paper: When explaining the error, you can now discuss HTTP 403 vs HTTP 401 . 401 Unauthorized: "I don't know who you are (Login failed)." 403 Forbidden: "I know who you are, but you aren't allowed here (Firewall/Permissions)." Would you like me to write a short paragraph explaining the difference between 401 and 403 errors for your "Status Codes" section?

Thunder Client is an excellent choice for your CIRAT lab demonstration because it keeps everything inside VS Code, reinforcing the "efficiency" and "developer-friendly" themes of your paper. It avoids the heavy memory usage of standalone apps like Postman. Here is a step-by-step guide to setting up a live demonstration. Phase 1: Preparation (Do this before the demo) Install the Extension: Open VS Code. Click the Extensions icon (square blocks on the left sidebar). Search for "Thunder Client" (created by Ranga Vadhineni). Click Install . Note: You will see a lightning bolt icon appear in your sidebar. Select a Test API: For a live demo, do not use a complex private API that might break. Use a reliable public test API. Recommendation: Use https://reqres.in/ . It is designed specifically for testing frontend/backend interactions. Phase 2: The Live Demonstration Steps Perform these actions to show how a client interacts with a server. Step 1: The GET Request (Retrieving Data) Explain: "First, we will simulate a client asking the server for a list of users." Click the Thunder Client (lightning bolt) icon. Click New Request . Ensure the dropdown says GET . Enter this URL: https://reqres.in/api/users?page=2 Click Send . Highlight the Result: Show the JSON response on the right panel. Talking Point: "Notice the status 200 OK . The server accepted our request and returned the data in JSON format, which is easy for our application to read." Step 2: The POST Request (Sending Data) Explain: "Now, we will simulate a user signing up or creating a new profile. This requires sending data to the server." Click New Request again. Change the dropdown from GET to POST . Enter this URL: https://reqres.in/api/users Click the Body tab (below the URL bar). Select JSON . Paste this simple JSON object: {
  "name": "CIRAT Analyst",
  "job": "Intelligence Aggregation"
} Click Send . Highlight the Result: Show the status 201 Created (Different from 200!). Show the response body, which will include a timestamp and a unique id . Talking Point: "The server processed our data, created a new resource, and gave us a confirmation ID. This is how a user registration system works behind the scenes." Optional Phase 3: Auth Simulation (Advanced) If you want to show off the "Access Token" concept you wrote about: In your POST request, click the Auth tab. Select Bearer Token . Type random-token-string-123 . Talking Point: "In a real scenario, I would paste my secure token here. This tells the server I have permission to make this change." Next Step: Would you like me to generate a caption or brief description for the screenshot you will take of this process, to include in your paper?

Based on the Elastic Common Schema (ECS) that winlogbeat uses to normalize data, the ProcessID is displayed in the following field: process.pid 💡 Why That Field? When you use an ELK stack, Winlogbeat collects the raw Windows event logs (like a PowerShell "Process Creation" event) and translates them into a standardized format called ECS . Raw Windows Log: The original Windows event might call the field NewProcessId (for Event ID 4688) or ProcessId (for Sysmon Event ID 1). Winlogbeat (ECS): Winlogbeat maps this to process.pid so that all of your data, regardless of its source, uses the same field name. In your Kibana event, you would see a structure similar to this: {
  "process": {
    "name": "powershell.exe",
    "executable": "C:\\Windows\\System32\\WindowsPowerShell\\v1.0\\powershell.exe",
    "command_line": "powershell -exec bypass -w hidden ...",
    "pid": 4736,  <-- This is the field
    "parent": {
      "pid": 1234
    }
  },
  "winlog": {
    "event_id": 1,
    "provider_name": "Microsoft-Windows-Sysmon"
  }
} This video explains how to configure Winlogbeat to monitor Windows events, which is the process that generates the logs you're seeing in Kibana. How to Configure Winlogbeat for Windows Logs This video is relevant because it shows the installation and configuration of Winlogbeat, the tool responsible for collecting the Windows event logs (like the PowerShell one) and forwarding them to your ELK stack.

